/-
# MULTIPURPOSE-MACHINE UNIFORM PARALLEL MACHINE SCHEDULING `QMPM|chains;p_i=1|C_max` is
# NP-complete

Source:
P. Brucker and S. Knust, *Complexity results for scheduling problems*, Universität Osnabrück,
class "Multipurpose machine problems" (`QMPM|chains;p_i=1|C_max`,
minimal NP-hard in the strong sense, citing W. Kubiak, "Exact and approximate algorithms for
scheduling unit time tasks with tree-like precedence constraints", Abstracts EURO IX - TIMS
XXVIII, Paris (1988), 195; that abstract was not available to us);
J. A. Hoogeveen, S. L. van de Velde and B. Veltman, "Complexity of scheduling multiprocessor
tasks with prespecified processor allocations", Discrete Applied Mathematics 55 (1994), 259-272,
doi:10.1016/0166-218X(94)90012-4, Theorem 2.7 (`P2|chain, fix_j, p_j = 1|C_max`, the case of
two machines of speed `1` and one-machine sets);
P. Brucker, *Scheduling Algorithms*, 5th ed., Springer (2007), Chapter 10 (multi-purpose
machines).
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Defs
public import MathlibExt.Computability.BinaryNatEncoding
public import MathlibExt.Computability.Complexity
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Rat
public import WantedExt.Complexity.NPComplete.MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespanWanted
public import WantedExt.Complexity.NPComplete.MultipurposeParallelTwoMachinesUnitChainsMakespanWanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespan

open MultiPurposeJobShopThreeJobs (Operation opLength eligible)
open UnitFlowShopPrecedenceMakespan (Prec)
open MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespan (MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespanInstance)

/-!
# MULTIPURPOSE-MACHINE UNIFORM PARALLEL MACHINE SCHEDULING `QMPM|chains;p_i=1|C_max` is NP-complete

INSTANCE: `m` uniform parallel machines `M_1, …, M_m` (`m` part of the input) with positive
rational speeds `s_1, …, s_m`, each processing at most one job at a time; jobs `1, …, n`, each
with processing time `p_i = 1` and a nonempty set `μ_i ⊆ {M_1, …, M_m}` of eligible machines;
precedence constraints that form a set of chains (every job has at most one immediate
predecessor and at most one immediate successor), where `i` preceding `j` means that `j` cannot
start before `i` is completed; and an integer `K`. Job `i` is processed without interruption on
exactly one machine `M_k ∈ μ_i`, which takes `p_i / s_k` time units. QUESTION: is there a
feasible schedule whose makespan `max_i C_i` is at most `K`, where `C_i` is the completion time
of job `i`?

This is `QMPM|chains;p_i=1|C_max` in the notation of Brucker's *Scheduling Algorithms*
(Chapter 10: every job `i` has a set `μ_i` of machines and is processed by exactly one machine of
`μ_i`; `Q` stands for uniform machines, on which `M_k` processes job `i` in `p_i / s_k` time)
and of the Brucker-Knust classification, which lists it as NP-hard in the strong sense, crediting
Kubiak (1988). NP-hardness also follows from a published proof: with `m = 2` and both speeds
`1` it is `PMPM2|chains;p_i=1|C_max`
(`WantedExt.Complexity.NPComplete.MultipurposeParallelTwoMachinesUnitChainsMakespanWanted`),
which contains the problem `P2|chain, fix_j, p_j = 1|C_max` that Hoogeveen, van de Velde and
Veltman (1994, Theorem 2.7) prove NP-hard in the strong sense (one-machine sets); with speed `1`
and integral data, the earliest start times for a fixed assignment and machine order are
integral, so rational start times give no shorter schedules.

An instance is a `PMPM|prec;p_i=1|C_max` instance (`MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespanInstance`
of `WantedExt.Complexity.NPComplete.MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespanWanted`: the number `m`
of machines, numbered `1, …, m`, the jobs as lists of operations `(p_i, μ_i)`, the bound `K`,
and the precedence arcs `(i, k)`) together with the list of speeds, the `k`-th pair `(a, b)`
giving `s_k = a / b` (the convention of `MultiprocessorSchedulingSpeedFactorsWanted`, whose
`speed` is indexed by the speeds of its own instance type). `WellFormed` asks for
that module's well-formedness (`m ≥ 1`, every job one operation of processing time `1` with a
nonempty machine set in `{1, …, m}`, arcs that name jobs and form an acyclic digraph), the chain
condition `IsChains` of `MultipurposeParallelTwoMachinesUnitChainsMakespanWanted` (on the same
arcs: every job has at most one immediate predecessor and at most one immediate successor in
the order generated by the arcs), exactly `m` speeds, and `0 < a`, `0 < b` for each speed. Job
`i` precedes job `k` when `k` is reachable from `i` along arcs. Every job is listed explicitly.

A schedule gives each job `t` a machine `a(t) ∈ μ_t` and a start time `σ(t) ∈ ℚ`, `σ(t) ≥ 0`;
then `C_t = σ(t) + p_t / s_{a(t)}`; two different jobs on the same machine occupy disjoint
intervals `[σ(t), C_t)` (the condition `IsNonOverlapping` of
`UniformMachinesReleaseMakespanWanted`, which is stated for the jobs of a
`MultiprocessorSchedulingSpeedFactors` instance and so is restated here for MPM jobs), and
`C_t ≤ σ(t')` for every arc `(t, t')`. Start times are rational, since processing times
`1 / s_k` are. The bound `K` is a natural number (a negative `K` gives only no-instances).
NP membership: a certificate is the machine of each job and the job order on each machine;
starting each job as early as possible gives start times that are sums of at most `n`
processing times `1 / s_k`, of size polynomial in the input. Binary encoding of all numbers.

Encoding: the instance is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's
canonical `MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`. Undecodable bitstrings, and
bitstrings decoding to malformed instances, are no-instances.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

/-- An instance: a `PMPM|prec;p_i=1|C_max` instance (the number `m` of machines, the jobs as lists
of operations `(p_i, μ_i)`, the bound `K`, the precedence arcs) and the speeds, the `k`-th pair
`(a, b)` being the speed `s_k = a / b` of machine `M_k`. -/
abbrev MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanInstance :=
  MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespanInstance × List (ℕ × ℕ)

/-- The speed `s_k = a / b` of machine `M_k`, `k ∈ {1, …, m}`, from the `k`-th listed pair
`(a, b)`. -/
def speed (i : MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanInstance) (k : ℕ) : ℚ :=
  ((i.2.getD (k - 1) (1, 1)).1 : ℚ) / (i.2.getD (k - 1) (1, 1)).2

/-- The completion time `C_t = σ(t) + p_t / s_{a(t)}` of job `t` started at time `σ(t)` on
machine `a(t)`. -/
def completion (i : MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanInstance)
    (a : Operation i.1.1 → ℕ) (σ : Operation i.1.1 → ℚ) (t : Operation i.1.1) : ℚ :=
  σ t + (opLength i.1.1 t : ℚ) / speed i (a t)

/-- Well-formedness of an instance: a well-formed `PMPM|prec;p_i=1|C_max` instance (`m ≥ 1`,
every job one operation of processing time `1` with a nonempty machine set in `{1, …, m}`, arcs
that name jobs and form an acyclic digraph) whose arcs form a set of chains, with exactly `m`
speeds, each `a / b` with `0 < a` and `0 < b`. -/
def WellFormed (i : MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanInstance) : Prop :=
  MultipurposeParallelPrecedenceMakespan.WellFormed i.1 ∧
    MultipurposeParallelTwoMachinesUnitChainsMakespan.IsChains i.1 ∧
    i.2.length = i.1.1.1 ∧ ∀ s ∈ i.2, 0 < s.1 ∧ 0 < s.2

/-- Some schedule, a machine `a(t) ∈ μ_t` and a start time `σ(t) ≥ 0` for each job `t`, has
makespan at most `K`: two different jobs on the same machine do not overlap, `C_t ≤ σ(t')` for
every arc `(t, t')`, and every job completes by `K`. -/
def MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanFeasible
    (i : MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanInstance) : Prop :=
  ∃ (a : Operation i.1.1 → ℕ) (σ : Operation i.1.1 → ℚ),
    (∀ t, a t ∈ eligible i.1.1 t) ∧ (∀ t, 0 ≤ σ t) ∧
    (∀ t t', t ≠ t' → a t = a t' →
      completion i a σ t ≤ σ t' ∨ completion i a σ t' ≤ σ t) ∧
    (∀ t t' : Operation i.1.1, Prec i.1.2 t.1 t'.1 → completion i a σ t ≤ σ t') ∧
    ∀ t, completion i a σ t ≤ i.1.1.2.2

open Classical in
/-- `QMPM|chains;p_i=1|C_max` as a decision problem over bitstrings. -/
noncomputable def multipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanDec :
    MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem := fun x =>
  match MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding.bitDecode
      (α := MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanInstance) x with
  | some i => decide (WellFormed i ∧ MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanFeasible i)
  | none => false

/-- **`QMPM|chains;p_i=1|C_max` is NP-complete.** Source: Brucker and Knust's classification
(Kubiak 1988); Hoogeveen, van de Velde and Veltman (1994), Theorem 2.7, via the case of two
machines of speed `1`. -/
public theorem_wanted multipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespan_npComplete :
    multipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanDec ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
      ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
        L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
          Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L multipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespanDec

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.MultipurposeUniformMachinesUnitChainsMakespan
